# modules/cloud/aws/live/checks-rds.rules - the §8.1 RDS read-only
# script-check registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; docs/STEP6-CLOUD-PLAN.md
# CLOUD-15).  Owned by modules/cloud/aws/live/rds.sh; ids are all
# `CLOUD-RDS-*`.
#
# A SEPARATE FILE FROM `checks.rules`, RATHER THAN APPENDED TO IT, per that
# file's own header: "A later service whose peers really are in flight
# simultaneously may add its own checks-<service>.rules beside this one; both
# spellings load, because checks_registry_load globs every *.rules under
# modules/cloud/ at any depth with no per-file allowlist and no registration
# step." Step 6's tier-2 service PRs (docs/DESIGN.md §13; the dispatch plan's
# own tier-2 bundle) are dispatched as genuinely parallel, simultaneously
# in-flight peers rather than the sequential bundles `checks.rules`'s own
# header describes - so this ticket takes the escape hatch that header names
# rather than appending to a file every sibling PR would also be appending to.
#
# `coverage-scope: account-region` is CLOUD's cell (§9.5.1). UNLIKE S3, EVERY
# RECORD BELOW LANDS IN THE `<account_id>/<region>` CELL, NOT `<account_id>/
# global`: `_CLOUD_SERVICES` marks `live/rds.sh` `regional` (RDS has no
# S3-shaped global namespace call - every instance and snapshot lives in
# exactly one region and is addressed by a regional endpoint), so the cell the
# pass actually visits and the region a finding cites are the SAME value here.
# See modules/cloud/aws/live/rds.sh's own header for the full reasoning.
#
# `cis:` IS AUTHORED HERE, ON THE RECORD, AND ONLY WHERE A REAL CIS v3.0.0
# CONTROL EXISTS (data/cis-mappings is an id -> label table and never a source
# of ids, docs/CIS-MAPPINGS.md §1). CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark v3.0.0
# section 2.3 - the RDS section - contains exactly three controls: 2.3.1
# (encryption at rest), 2.3.2 (auto minor version upgrade, which no check
# below observes), and 2.3.3 (public access). CLOUD-RDS-NO_ENCRYPTION-01 and
# CLOUD-RDS-PUBLIC_ACCESS-01 cite those two; CLOUD-RDS-NO_BACKUPS-01 and
# CLOUD-RDS-PUBLIC_SNAPSHOT-01 cite NOTHING, because v3.0.0 has no RDS backup-
# retention or snapshot-exposure control - the identical honest-absence
# decision modules/cloud/aws/live/checks.rules already documents for S3's
# versioning and logging checks. Inventing a control number to satisfy a "must
# cite CIS" habit is the overstated-coverage failure docs/DESIGN.md §15
# forbids.
#
# The type tag is `config-read` on every record (one read-only AWS API call,
# mutates nothing), plus `quick` (docs/DESIGN.md §5's "passive + config-read,
# no active probes"), plus `compliance` on exactly the two records that carry
# a `cis` value (finding F3's TAG reading of that profile, lib/checks.sh) -
# the identical scheme checks.rules already uses for S3.

id: CLOUD-RDS-PUBLIC_ACCESS-01
title: RDS instance is configured as publicly accessible
script: aws/live/rds.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 2.3.3
remediation: Set the instance's PubliclyAccessible setting to false so it no
  longer receives a public IP address or a publicly resolvable DNS name. A
  publicly accessible instance is reachable from outside its VPC subject only
  to its security group rules, which makes a single overly permissive
  security group rule the whole remaining barrier between this database and
  the internet. Reach the instance through a bastion host, a VPN, or an
  application tier running inside the same VPC instead, and review CloudTrail
  and the database's own connection logs for any access from an unexpected
  source while it was public.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_VPC.html

id: CLOUD-RDS-NO_ENCRYPTION-01
title: RDS instance storage is not encrypted at rest
script: aws/live/rds.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-311
owasp: A02:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
tags: compliance
coverage-scope: account-region
cis: 2.3.1
remediation: Encryption at rest can only be enabled at CREATION time for an
  RDS instance - it cannot be turned on for an existing one in place. Create
  an encrypted snapshot of the instance (copy-db-snapshot with
  --kms-key-id, or a manual snapshot followed by a copy that sets encryption),
  restore a new instance from that encrypted snapshot, cut applications over
  to it, and decommission the unencrypted original. Once encrypted, its
  automated backups, read replicas and further snapshots inherit encryption
  automatically.
references: CWE-311
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.Encryption.html

id: CLOUD-RDS-NO_BACKUPS-01
title: RDS instance has automated backups and point-in-time recovery disabled
script: aws/live/rds.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-693
owasp: A05:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Set the instance's backup retention period to a non-zero value
  (7-35 days is the ordinary range). This enables both automated daily
  backups and point-in-time recovery, since PITR is built on the automated
  backup plus the transaction-log stream - a retention period of zero
  disables both at once. Without either, an accidental deletion, a bad
  migration, or a compromised credential that damages this database's data is
  unrecoverable except from a manual snapshot taken before the incident, if
  one happens to exist.
references: CWE-693
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_WorkingWithAutomatedBackups.html

id: CLOUD-RDS-PUBLIC_SNAPSHOT-01
title: RDS snapshot is shared publicly with every AWS account
script: aws/live/rds.sh
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-284
owasp: A01:2021
tags: config-read
tags: quick
coverage-scope: account-region
remediation: Remove the public share immediately
  (modify-db-snapshot-attribute --db-snapshot-identifier ID
  --attribute-name restore --values-to-remove all). A snapshot's restore
  attribute set to 'all' is AWS's own documented sentinel for "any AWS
  account in this partition may restore a full copy of this database from
  it" - not a named account, every account - so this is the entire database
  as of the snapshot's creation time, exposed to anyone who can guess or
  enumerate the snapshot identifier. Review whether the snapshot was already
  restored by an account you did not authorise, and confirm no other
  snapshot of this or a related instance carries the same attribute.
references: CWE-284
references: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_ShareSnapshot.html
